{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AFiA9LRfCqZ5vLvoi4xKit","type":"tool_result","content":"10\tFor each of the six ambiguity types, we construct 50 \\emph{clause pairs}: an ambiguous variant and a matched unambiguous variant that conveys the same intended policy but resolves the ambiguity. Pairs are minimal edits: the unambiguous variant adds only the information necessary to eliminate the target ambiguity (e.g., for scopal ambiguity, replacing ``items may be returned within 30 days'' with ``standard items may be returned within 30 days; customized items are final sale''). This yields 300 clause pairs $\\times$ 2 conditions $\\times$ 5 models $= 2{,}997$ episodes (3 episodes lost to API errors in one Qwen3 batch).\n11\t\n12\tClause pairs are manually constructed using structured templates and reviewed by two independent annotators (both with NLP background) to ensure (1) the ambiguous variant contains exactly one instance of the target type, (2) the unambiguous variant resolves it without changing policy scope, and (3) the ambiguity is plausibly natural. Annotators independently verified type assignments with a 96\\% initial agreement (287/300; Cohen's $\\kappa = 0.95$ on six-type classification); this high agreement reflects annotation precision---each pair differs only in the ambiguity-introducing edit---not task simplicity. The 13 disagreements were resolved by discussion, yielding 100\\% final consensus. A post-hoc realism validation confirms linguistic consistency with original $\\tau^2$-bench policy text (\\S\\ref{sec:limitations}).\n13\t\n14\t\\subsection{Models}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BfYwh1nka74n7vxVPZy8Vi","session_id":"2ca9953f-3689-48c5-81f7-171ddea5ca88","uuid":"8665e05c-37ec-4a00-8392-b7f24fce6ccb","timestamp":"2026-05-23T19:54:23.721Z"}
